<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      28-01. `pos.terminals.admin` — the permission that decides which POS TERMINALS a branch has.

      ══ What a terminal is ══

      Phase 28 (D-28-03) makes a POS terminal a named profile rather than "whatever browser is
      open": a name, a branch, the set of stations it fires to, and the menu categories it offers.
      That is what "a dedicated POS selecting the respective menu" means, and creating, renaming,
      re-scoping and retiring one is catalogue state — the same class of decision as deciding which
      dining tables the restaurant has.

      ══ Who holds it ══

      OWNER, TENANT_ADMIN, MANAGER — exactly the holders of `pos.tables.admin` (083), and for the
      same argument that changeset makes. A branch manager already owns the menu (`pos.menu.manage`)
      and the floor plan (`pos.tables.admin`); deciding how many tills the branch runs and what each
      one offers is the same kind of decision, made by the same person, on the same day the branch
      opens.

      CASHIER and WAITER hold NEITHER this nor anything that implies it. A cashier USES a terminal;
      they do not get to re-scope the menu it offers. That distinction is the whole reason this is a
      separate code rather than a reuse of `pos.orders.create`.

      ══ Why the grant is explicit ══

      Granted EXPLICITLY, never via a catalogue-wide SELECT from `permissions`. Every blanket grant
      in this changelog (030/041/042/045/046/057) is point-in-time: it enumerates what existed the
      day it ran and retro-grants nothing added afterwards. 055, 059, 082 and 083 all say this and
      all grant explicitly for the same reason.

      A permission that no role holds produces a clean, confident 403 for every user including
      OWNER — indistinguishable from a deliberate denial, and invisible in logs. That is the exact
      defect class `PermissionCatalogClosureTest` exists to catch, which is why it scans
      @PreAuthorize across every service rather than trusting review, and why the verification
      changeSet below RAISEs rather than warns.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-085-pos-terminals-admin" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>pos.terminals.admin — POS terminal profile CRUD (name, station set, menu scope), held by the same roles as pos.tables.admin</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('pos.terminals.admin', 'pos', 'Create, rename, re-scope, retire and reactivate POS terminal profiles (catalogue, not order entry)')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'pos.terminals.admin'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'pos.terminals.admin'),
              ('MANAGER',      'pos.terminals.admin')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'pos.terminals.admin';
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'pos.terminals.admin';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      Says so if the grant did not land, rather than leaving a permission that exists and that
      nobody holds — which is invisible at runtime (everyone including OWNER just gets a clean 403
      on the Terminals screen) and is precisely how the five outages listed in
      PermissionCatalogClosureTest's javadoc each reached production. Same RAISE posture as 056,
      057, 082 and 083.
    -->
    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-085-verify-pos-terminals-admin" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>Fail the migration if pos.terminals.admin exists but no role holds it</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE holder_count INT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT count(*) INTO holder_count
                  FROM role_permissions
                 WHERE permission_code = 'pos.terminals.admin';
                IF holder_count = 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'pos.terminals.admin is defined but granted to no role. PosTerminalController gates create/update/activate/deactivate on it, so the POS terminal management screen would 403 for every user on every tenant including OWNER - and it would do so as a normal-looking permission denial, not an error anyone would trace back to this migration.';
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
